### Former Wonnenstein Monastery

After Gorge Number Two and a prison, you pass a former nuns’ convent.
The convent was only dissolved in 2021 due to a lack of new recruits.

One might wonder how a Catholic monastery fits into the Protestant canton of Appenzell <em>Ausserrhoden</em> — after all, the half-cantons are historically divided along denominational lines.

The monastery provides information on this via information boards:
Although the monastery’s status remained unclear for a long time, several decades after the original Protestant uprisings, it was agreed that everything within the monastery walls officially belongs to Appenzell <em>Innerrhoden</em>. The monastery is therefore a tiny enclave of Appenzell Innerrhoden within Appenzell Ausserrhoden.


<HikeImage idx={34} />

The monastery church itself has only recently been restored, with a new altar for <i>ad populum</i> Masses.
The three altars behind it all focus on the Holy Family, as the church is dedicated to the Blessed Virgin Mary.
The statue of the Virgin Mary on the left-hand altar may look familiar to Swiss Catholics.
It is a replica of the Black Madonna of Einsiedeln.
Accordingly, the replica was also produced following the unsuccessful restoration of the Einsiedeln Madonna, which had originally been painted in colour.


The final stretch before Teufen runs partly alongside the motorway, which isn’t ideal. Thankfully, though, it’s all over after 15 minutes.
<HikeImage idx={35} />

## Arrival in Teufen

Teufen can be reached via the aptly named village <q>of Einsamkeit</q>, where a single, beautiful farmhouse welcomes you.
<HikeImage idx={36} />
<HikeImage idx={37} />
<HikeImage idx={38} />

## Journey home

A train runs every half hour from Teufen to St. Gallen. From there, take the appropriate connection home.

